###############################################################################
# Set default behavior to automatically normalize line endings.
#
# This is a Windows-only project and several consumers REQUIRE CRLF in the
# working tree regardless of core.autocrlf:
#   - SQLScriptParser splits DB scripts on \r\n\r\n (LF scripts yield
#     "Found no SQL commands" and database creation fails)
#   - RegressionTests Resources\*.eml contain DKIM-signed messages whose body
#     hashes break if line endings change
#   - Raw-message tests send file/string content verbatim over SMTP; bare LFs
#     are rejected with "554 Message containing bare LF's"
# Hence: normalize text in the repo, but ALWAYS check out CRLF.
###############################################################################
* text=auto eol=crlf

###############################################################################
# The exception: everything a POSIX machine EXECUTES.
#
# The rule above is right for a Windows-only project and wrong for the moment
# one of its files is run by /bin/sh. A maintainer script checked out with CRLF
# fails as "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M" when the package is installed, and the
# failure is at the user's machine rather than here. The systemd unit, the
# logrotate rule and the packaged configuration are read by parsers that are
# equally unforgiving, and the AppImage and PKGBUILD are shell.
#
# These are checked out LF on every platform, including Windows, where nothing
# reads them but an editor.
###############################################################################
hmailserver/source/Server/platform/packaging/** text eol=lf
build/*.sh text eol=lf

# Explicitly binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.pfx binary
*.snk binary
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.bmp binary
*.msi binary
*.tlb binary
*.7z  binary
*.zip binary

